WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.441
The secret lifecycle starts before application code reads a value.

2
00:00:04.441 --> 00:00:19.323
A secret is created for a purpose, stored in a location, granted to a workload or person, used by code, rotated on a schedule or after an event, audited for access, revoked when trust changes, and deleted when it is no longer needed.

3
00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:28.050
Each phase is a chance to reduce risk or accidentally expand it. Hardcoding a secret is dangerous even when the repository is private.

4
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.699
Source control is optimized for memory.

5
00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:40.673
Git history, forks, pull request diffs, code review tools, local clones, backups, and build systems can preserve a value long after the line is removed.

6
00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:46.984
Once a secret enters source control, the right response is usually rotation, not just deletion.

7
00:00:46.984 --> 00:00:55.555
Removing the visible string may clean the current file, but it does not make old commits, build logs, or developer machines forget.

8
00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.438
Secret exposure also happens outside code.

9
00:00:58.438 --> 00:01:07.087
A Go service can leak a database password through a formatted error, a panic, a test fixture, a golden file, or a structured log field.

10
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:16.826
A CI job can print environment variables during debugging. A Dockerfile can copy a secret into a build layer. A crash dump can contain process memory.

11
00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:24.618
A binary or container image can carry embedded configuration if the build process substituted values at compile time.

12
00:01:24.618 --> 00:01:34.747
These paths matter because attackers and incident responders both look beyond the current source file. Ownership makes lifecycle management possible.

13
00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:44.253
Every secret should have a purpose, an owner, an environment, a permission boundary, and a rotation path. Purpose answers why the secret exists.

14
00:01:44.253 --> 00:01:52.980
Owner answers who can approve changes and respond to exposure. Environment separates development, test, staging, and production.

15
00:01:52.980 --> 00:02:01.317
Permission boundary defines what the value can do. Rotation path explains how to replace it without guessing during an outage.

16
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:04.901
For Go engineers, lifecycle thinking changes design.

17
00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:13.706
Instead of passing a raw string through many packages, you define where the value enters the program and which dependency receives it.

18
00:02:13.706 --> 00:02:19.861
Instead of letting missing values fall back to insecure defaults, you fail closed at startup.

19
00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:25.316
Instead of logging full configuration for convenience, you implement redaction.

20
00:02:25.316 --> 00:02:35.211
Instead of assuming a database password is permanent, you design for rotation and connection refresh. The lifecycle is not paperwork around the code.

21
00:02:35.211 --> 00:02:41.678
It is part of the system behavior that keeps a service recoverable when credentials change or leak.

22
00:02:41.678 --> 00:02:47.210
A useful lifecycle review also asks where the secret might be copied automatically.

23
00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:57.885
Build caches, deployment manifests, container registries, crash reporting tools, and observability pipelines may all receive data from the application path.

24
00:02:57.885 --> 00:03:03.651
The safest teams assume that a secret can travel unless the system is designed to stop it.

25
00:03:03.651 --> 00:03:11.988
That assumption leads to better rotation procedures, cleaner access reviews, and fewer surprises during incident response.
